@page
@{
	ViewData["Title"] = "About";
}

<main class="about-page">
	<h1>About legit-content</h1>

	<p>
		legit-content is a small, human-curated feed of links to content that is
		actually worth your time: articles, videos, podcasts, courses, books and
		tools. No infinite scroll, no engagement bait, no brain-rot — just things
		people found useful and vouched for.
	</p>

	<h2>How the feed works</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>Every link is posted by a person with a short note on why it is worth our time.</li>
		<li>Upvotes decide what stays visible. New posts also get a small freshness boost.</li>
		<li>A post is always visible during its first 30 days. After that it only stays if at least one person upvoted it.</li>
		<li>You can sort by <strong>Most legit</strong> or <strong>Newest</strong>, search titles and descriptions, and filter by type or nested tags like <code>medicine/anatomy</code>.</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>Using the site</h2>
	<ul>
		<li><strong>Browse</strong> — open the feed and read what looks interesting. Use the search icon in the top bar for search, type filters, sort and tags.</li>
		<li><strong>Upvote</strong> — the ▲ button on a card. Click again to undo. Voting requires an account.</li>
		<li><strong>Add a link</strong> — the + button (bottom left) opens the add dialog. Paste a URL, give it a title, explain why it is worth our time, pick a type and up to five tags.</li>
		<li><strong>Mark as read</strong> — the ✓ button on a card hides it from your feed so new content stands out. Everything you marked lives on the <em>Already read</em> page, where you can unmark it.</li>
		<li><strong>Archive</strong> — the bookmark button saves a title + URL snapshot that outlives the feed. Find and delete saved items on the <em>Archive</em> page.</li>
		<li><strong>Menu</strong> — the ☰ button (top right) holds the feed, this page, your archive and already-read lists, plus your account controls.</li>
		<li><strong>Theme</strong> — the ◐ button switches between light and dark.</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>Posting rules</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>Post content you genuinely think is good, not something you want clicks on.</li>
		<li>Write a real explanation of why it is worth our time — that note is required.</li>
		<li>One link per URL; duplicates are rejected.</li>
		<li>Up to five links per person per day.</li>
		<li>Use a descriptive title and specific tags so other people can find it.</li>
	</ul>

	<p class="muted">curated by humans, for humans — no feed-farming, no brain-rot.</p>
</main>
